ell around 93-95%", "Powell has out-raised Harding and drawn more outside support"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point clearly to Democrats: the district leans D by 3 points, the national environment adds roughly 8-9 more, and Powell has a large fundraising and outside-spending edge over Harding. Raters overwhelmingly call it a Democratic flip and markets price Powell near 93-95%, so a Likely D rating with a double-digit expected margin fits the evidence.", "watch": "A late shift toward Harding on affordability and immigration themes, or stronger-than-expected Republican turnout in the Omaha suburbs, could narrow the margin.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Don Bacon (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's 2nd District is an open seat with Don Bacon retiring, removing a Republican incumbent's personal advantage in a district that already leans slightly Democratic. The 2026 national environment favors Democrats, and Denise Powell has out-raised Brinker Harding by a wide margin while drawing substantially more outside spending on her behalf. Polling is sparse but shows Powell ahead, with the most recent large survey giving her a 12-point lead.\n\nExpert raters uniformly expect a Democratic flip, ranging from Lean D to Safe D, and prediction markets put Powell's win probability in the low-to-mid 90s. Harding has emphasized his experience and the race has drawn heavy attention, but the weight of evidence points to a Likely Democratic pickup rather than a true tossup.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9278, "margin": 8.8}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9035, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.914, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9146, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8905, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9055, "margin": 8.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.902, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.8848, "margin": 7.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30